    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Several researchers have suggested that perturbed stress fields associated with active faults significantly affect flow properties in a fractured basement reservoir. To examine this insight quantitatively, however, it is required to introduce both a geomechanical model for estimating perturbed stress fields by slip on faults and a fracture mechanics model for describing fracture behavior in response to changing certain stress conditions. We combine an elastic dislocation model of active faults and a simple stress-compliant function for fracture apertures to evaluate flow properties quantitatively in the Yufutsu fractured basement reservoir, Japan.\r\n\r\nThe observed flow properties are apparently affected by perturbed stress fields, which presumably are associated with active faults. The most productive well in the reservoir shows significant rotations of the azimuths of horizontal stresses along the well path, whereas the directions of horizontal stresses at non-productive wells usually are oriented consistently. As for fracture behaviors, step-rate hydraulic injection tests at the non-productive well shows that fracture apertures open significantly as the effective minimum compressive stress approaches zero. These observations indicate an important effect of perturbed stress fields on flow properties.\r\n\r\nAs the result of geomechanial modeling, the observed stress orientation profiles can be reproduced by the model. Non-productive wells were drilled in the area where the regional stress state prevails, and the most productive well penetrates the area near the tips of active faults, where the stress concentration leads to enhancements in fracture permeability by opening or shearing. The geomechanical model combined with the stress-compliant function for opening of fracture apertures, which is estimated from the injection test, can explain the observed difference of well performances to first order. The model demonstrates high potential of fracture permeability created by perturbed stress fields.",
